s results.\n\nTherefore, the product is 71,487.\n\nWait, but let me confirm once more with another method, perhaps vertically:\n\nMultiplying 611 by 117:\n\n          611  \n        \u00d7117  \n        ------  \n          4277  (611 \u00d77)  \n         6110   (611 \u00d710, shifted one position)  \n        61100   (611 \u00d7100, shifted two positions)  \n        ------  \n        71487  \n\nAdding the three lines:\n\nFirst line: 4,277\n\nSecond line: 6,110 \u2192 total so far: 4,277 +6,110 =10,387\n\nThird line: 61,100 \u2192 total 10,387 +61,100 =71,487. Exactly.\n\nSo I think this is correct. The key was realizing I misread the original problem first, but after correcting that, the multiplication is straightforward and cross-verified with multiple methods.\n\nTherefore, the product of 611 and 117 is 71,487.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 611 and 117 is \\boxed{71487}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 611 and 117, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n      611 \\\\\n    \\times 117 \\\\\n    \\hline\n      4277  \\quad \\text{(611 \u00d7 7)} \\\\\n     6110   \\quad \\text{(611 \u00d7 10, shifted one position)} \\\\\n    61100   \\quad \\text{(611 \u00d7 100, shifted two positions)} \\\\\n    \\hline\n    71487 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down step-by-step:\n\n1. Multiply 611 by 7:\n   \\[\n   611 \\times 7 = 4277\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 611 by 10 (shifted one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   611 \\times 10 = 6110\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 611 by 100 (shifted two positions to the left):\n   \\[\n   611 \\times 100 = 61100\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the results of the three multiplications:\n   \\[\n   4277 + 6110 + 61100 = 71487\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 611 and 117 is \\(\\boxed{71487}\\).",
