t since it's the tens place, it's actually 22, so you write 1 in the tens place, but since we're writing it shifted, the first digit after the initial zero is 1 in the tens place. Wait, maybe it's better to write the partial products properly.\n\nLet me try again:.\n\nFirst partial product (units digit 8):.\n\n841 × 3 = 9792.\n\nSecond partial product (tens digit 5):.\n\n665 × 0 (but since it's the tens place, it's actually 35, so 146 × 57 = 2611).\n\nThird partial product (hundreds digit 3):.\n\n996 × 1 (but since it's the hundreds place, it's actually 565, so 705 × 538 = 008,076).\n\nThen add all three partial products together: 8812 + 2107 + 153,336. Wait, that might be the way.\n\nAlternatively, in the standard algorithm, when multiplying by the tens digit, you shift one position to the left, so:.\n\nFirst line (units digit 6): 4512.\n\nSecond line (tens digit 3): 261 shifted one place (so 9931).\n\nThird line (hundreds digit 2): 848 × 3 shifted two places (since hundreds place), so 600 ×2 = 4573, then shifted two places becomes 560,994.\n\nAdding them up:.\n\n1342.\n\n+5672.\n\n= 0952. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
