ong.\n\nAlternatively, let me try another approach. Maybe breaking down the numbers into smaller components.\n\n185 can be written as 200 - 15. So, 133 \u00d7 (200 - 15) = 133\u00d7200 - 133\u00d715.\n\nCompute 133\u00d7200 first: 133*2 = 266, so 266 \u00d7100 = 26,600.\n\nThen 133\u00d715: 133\u00d710 = 1,330 and 133\u00d75=665. Adding them gives 1,330 + 665 = 1,995.\n\nSubtracting: 26,600 - 1,995 = 24,605. Okay, so that matches the second method. So the correct answer is 24,605? Wait, but earlier when I did the standard algorithm, I thought of 24,790 but that must have been a miscalculation. Let me check again.\n\nWait, in my first standard algorithm attempt, I had 134 \u00d7 185? Wait, the original question was 216 and 398? Wait, hold on! Wait, the user asked for the product of 216 and 398, but in my thinking I was doing 133 and 185. Oh no! I must have misread the question. That's why my numbers were conflicting. So I need to start over with the correct numbers.\n\nThe user's question is: What is the product of 216 and 398. So, 216 \u00d7 398. Okay, my mistake earlier was thinking of different numbers. Let's do this correctly now.\n\nAlright, so 216 multiplied by 398. Let me use the standard algorithm properly this time.\n\nFirst, write down:\n\n      216  \n    \u00d7398  \n    ______\n\nStarting with the units place of the bottom number (398), which is 8.\n\nMultiply 216 \u00d7 8:  \n216 \u00d7 8:  \n6\u00d78=48, write down 8 and carryover 4.  \n1\u00d78=8 +4=12, write down 2 and carryover 1.  \n2\u00d78=16 +1=17.  \nSo that's 1,728.\n\nNext, multiply by the tens place (9), which is actually 90. So we need to shift one position to the left.\n\n216 \u00d7 9:  \n6\u00d79=54, write down 4, carryover 5.  \n1\u00d79=9 +5=14, write down 4, carryover 1.  \n2\u00d79=18 +1=19.  \nSo that's 1,944, but shifted one place left: 19,440.\n\nNow, multiply by the hundreds place (3), which is 300. So shift two positions left.\n\n216 \u00d7 3:  \n6\u00d73=18, write 8, carryover 1