d I must have confused the numbers. Probably I miscalculated the first step when breaking down 190 and 304.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be 507,378. Let me confirm with a calculator approach.\n\nAlternatively, 821 \u00d7 618:\n\nLet me compute 800\u00d7600 = 480,000\n\nThen, 800\u00d718 = 14,400\n\n21\u00d7600 = 12,600\n\n21\u00d718 = 378\n\nAdding all these parts: 480,000 +14,400 = 494,400; 494,400 +12,600 = 507,000; 507,000 +378 = 507,378. Yep, same result.\n\nSo the correct product is 507,378. The first method I must have miscalculated the breakdown, perhaps by using wrong numbers. It's easy to make an error when breaking down into parts if you don't keep track.\n\nTherefore, I should trust the standard algorithm here, which gives 507,378.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 821 and 618 is \\boxed{507378}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 821 and 618, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 821 by 8 (the units digit of 618):\n   \\[\n   821 \\times 8 = 6,568\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 821 by 10 (the tens digit of 618, shifted one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   821 \\times 10 = 8,210\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 821 by 600 (the hundreds digit of 618, shifted two positions to the left):\n   \\[\n   821 \\times 600 = 492,600\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the results of the three multiplications:\n   \\[\n   6,568 + 8,210 + 492,600\n   \\]\n\n   - First, add \\(6,568 + 8,210\\):\n     \\[\n     6,568 + 8,210 = 14,778\n     \\]\n\n   - Then add the result to \\(492,600\\):\n     \\[\n     14,778 + 492,600 = 507,378\n     \\]\n\nThus, the product of 821 and 618 is \\(\\boxed{507378}\\).",
