Breakdown 56 into 50 +6.\n\n274*50 = 13,700 (since 274*5=1,370, times 10 is 13,700).\n\n274*6 =1,644.\n\nAdding them: 13,700 +1,644 =15,344. Then multiply by10 gives 153,440. Same result.\n\nAlright, so after correcting my initial mistake, I think the correct answer is 153,440.\n\nWait, just to be absolutely sure, maybe do it step by step:\n\n274 x 560:\n\nLet me write it out vertically:\n\n       274  \n     x560  \n     -------\n        0   (274 x 0)\n     16440  (274 x 6, shifted one position)\n   +137000  (274 x5, shifted two positions)\n     -------\n     153440\n\nYes, adding 16,440 and 137,000 gives 153,440. So I think that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, using calculator-like steps:\n\n560 x 200 =112,000\n\n560 x70=39,200\n\n560 x4=2,240\n\nAdd them together: 112,000 +39,200 =151,200 +2,240=153,440. Yep, same result.\n\nTherefore, the correct product is 153,440. My first mistake was incorrectly considering the units digit as 5 instead of 0, but after correcting that, all methods converge to 153,440.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 274 and 560 is \\boxed{153440}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 274 and 560, we can break down the multiplication using the distributive property and the standard algorithm.\n\nFirst, we recognize that 560 can be decomposed into 500 + 60 + 0. \n\n1. **Units place (0):**\n   \\[\n   274 \\times 0 = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Tens place (60):**\n   \\[\n   274 \\times 60 = 274 \\times 6 \\times 10 = (200 \\times 6 + 70 \\times 6 + 4 \\times 6) \\times 10 = (1200 + 420 + 24) \\times 10 = 1644 \\times 10 = 16440\n   \\]\n\n3. **Hundreds place (500):**\n   \\[\n   274 \\times 500 = 274 \\times 5 \\times 100 = (200 \\times 5 + 70 \\times 5 + 4 \\times 5) \\times 100 = (1000 + 350 + 20) \\times 100 = 1370 \\times 100 = 137000\n   \\]\n\nAdding all these results together:\n\\[\n0 + 16440 + 137000 = 153440\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this by another method:\n- Breaking down 560 into 56 and multiplying by 10:\n  \\[\n  274 \\times 56 = 274 \\times (50 + 6) = 274 \\times 50 + 274 \\times 6 = 13700 + 1644 = 15344\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  15344 \\times 10 = 153440\n  \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 274 and 560 is \\(\\boxed{153440}\\).",
