0d75=25 +1=26, so write 26. So that gives 2,680, shifted left once becomes 26,800.\n\nThird line: multiply by 1 (hundreds place, shifted left twice):\n\n1\u00d76=6, so write 6 in the hundreds place? Wait, actually, since it's the hundreds digit, the entire number is shifted two places left. So 1\u00d7536 = 536, shifted two places is 53,600.\n\nAdding them all together: 3,752 + 26,800 = 30,552; then 30,552 + 53,600 = 84,152. Yep, that checks out. So the correct product is 84,152. I think I confused myself initially with different numbers, but breaking it down step by step clarifies it. So the answer is 84,152.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 536 and 157 is \\boxed{84152}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 536 and 157, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 157 into 100 + 50 + 7:\n\n1. Calculate \\(536 \\times 100\\):\n   \\[\n   536 \\times 100 = 53600\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(536 \\times 50\\):\n   \\[\n   536 \\times 50 = 26800\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(536 \\times 7\\):\n   \\[\n   536 \\times 7 = 3752\n   \\]\n\nAdd the results of these three calculations together:\n\\[\n53600 + 26800 = 80400\n\\]\n\\[\n80400 + 3752 = 84152\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n- Multiply 536 by 7: \\(536 \\times 7 = 3752\\)\n- Multiply 536 by 50 (shifted one position left): \\(536 \\times 50 = 26800\\)\n- Multiply 536 by 100 (shifted two positions left): \\(536 \\times 100 = 53600\\)\n\nAdding these results:\n\\[\n3752 + 26800 = 30552\n\\]\n\\[\n30552 + 53600 = 84152\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 536 and 157 is \\(\\boxed{84152}\\).",
