      "text": "89,426.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused here. Maybe I need to approach this more systematically.\n\nAlternative method: Use the distributive property (breaking down into hundreds, tens, and units).\n\nLet me denote the numbers as:\n\n1,167 = 1,000 + 100 + 60 + 7.\n\n8,745 = 8,000 + 700 + 40 + 5.\n\nThen, multiply each part:\n\nFirst, multiply 1,000 by 8,745: 8,745,000.\n\nThen, 100 \u00d7 8,745 = 874,500.\n\n60 \u00d7 8,745 = 524,700.\n\n7 \u00d7 8,745 = 61,215.\n\nNow, add all those together:\n\n8,745,000 + 874,500 = 9,619,500.\n\n9,619,500 + 524,700 = 10,144,200.\n\n10,144,200 + 61,215 = 10,205,415.\n\nHmm, so that's 10,205,415.\n\nWait a second, but earlier when I was trying the standard algorithm, I ended up with different numbers. Maybe I made a mistake there. Let me check again with the standard method step by step.\n\nMultiplying 1,167 by 8,745.\n\nFirst, write them down:\n\n\u2003\u2003\u2003\u2003\u2003\u2003\u20031,167.\n\u00d7\u2003\u2003\u2003\u2003\u2003\u2003\u20038,745.\n\u2003-------------------.\n\nMultiply 1,167 by each digit of 8,745 starting from the right.\n\nStarting with the units digit: 5.\n\n1,167 \u00d7 5:\n\n7 \u00d7 5 = 35. Write down 5, carryover 3.\n\n6 \u00d7 5 = 30 + 3 = 33. Write 3, carryover 3.\n\n1 \u00d7 5 = 5 + 3 = 8. Write 8.\n\n1 \u00d7 5 = 5. Write 5.\n\nSo the first partial product is 5,835.\n\nNext, the tens digit: 4 (which is actually 40).\n\nMultiply 1,167 \u00d7 4, but shifted one position to the left (because it's the tens place).\n\n7 \u00d7 4 = 28. Write 8, carryover 2.\n\n6 \u00d7 4 = 24 + 2 = 26. Write 6, carryover 2.\n\n1 \u00d7 4 = 4 + 2 = 6. Write 6.\n\n1 \u00d7 4 = 4. Write 4.\n\nSo that's 4,668 shifted one place to the left: 46,680.\n\nNext, the hundreds digit: 7 (which is 700).\n\nMultiply 1,167 \u00d7 7, shifted two positions left.\n\n7 \u00d7 7 = 49. Write 9, carryover 4.\n\n6 \u00d7 7 = 42 + 4 = 46. Write 6, carryover 4.\n\n1 \u00d7 7 = 7 + 4 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n1 \u00d7 7 = 7 + 1 = 8. Write 8.\n\nSo that's 8,169 shifted two places left: 816,900.\n\nFinally, the thousands digit: 8 (which is 8,000).\n\nMultiply 1,167 \u00d7 8, shifted three positions left.\n\n7 \u00d7 8 = 56. Write 6, carryover 5.\n\n6 \u00d7 8 = 48 + 5 = 53. Write 3, carryover 5.\n\n1 \u00d7 8 = 8 + 5 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n1 \u00d7 8 = 8 + 1 = 9. Write 9.\n\nResult is 9,336 shifted three places left: 9,336,000.\n\nNow, adding all the partial products:\n\nFirst partial product: 5,835.\n\nSecond: 46,680.\n\nThird: 816,900.\n\nFourth: 9,336,000.\n\nAdding them step by step:\n\nStart with 5,835 + 46,680 = 52,515.\n\n52,515 + 816,900 = 869,415.\n\n869,415 + 9,336,000 = 10,205,415.\n\nOkay, so that's consistent with the distributive method. So the correct product is 10,205,415. \n\nWait, but the original question was 829 multiplied by 522, not 1,167 and 8,745. Oh no! I think I misread the problem initially and started solving a different one. Let me check the original question again.\n\nThe user asked: \"What is the Product of 829 and 522?\" \n\nOh no, I completely messed up by solving 1,167 \u00d7 8,745 instead. That's a big mistake. I need to start over.\n\nAlright, let's tackle 829 \u00d7 522 correctly.\n\nFirst, I'll write them down:\n\n\u2003\u2003\u2003829\n\u00d7\u2003522\n--------\n\nStarting with the units digit of the bottom number (522's units digit is 2):\n\nMultiply 829 \u00d7 2.\n\n9 \u00d7 2 = 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\n2 \u00d7 2 = 4 + 1 = 5. Write 5.\n\n8 \u00d7 2 = 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nThe thousands place: 1 (carryover) so write 1.\n\nSo first partial product: 1,658.\n\nNext, multiply by the tens digit (2 in 522's tens place, so it's actually 20).\n\nSo 829 \u00d7 20. Let's compute 829 \u00d7 2 first and then add a zero at the end.\n\n829 \u00d7 2:\n\n9\u00d72=18, write 8 carryover 1.\n\n2\u00d72=4 +1=5.\n\n8\u00d72=16. So 1,658. Adding a zero gives 16,580. \n\nWait, but actually, since it's the tens digit, we should shift one position left, which is equivalent to multiplying by 10. So yes, that's 16,580.\n\nThird, multiply by the hundreds digit (5 in 522's hundreds place, which is 500).\n\n829 \u00d7 500. Let's compute 829 \u00d7 5 first and then add two zeros.\n\n829 \u00d7 5:\n\n9\u00d75=45. Write 5 carryover 4.\n\n2\u00d75=10 +4=14. Write 4 carryover 1.\n\n8\u00d75=40 +1=41. Write 1 carryover 4.\n\nNo more digits, so write the 4. So that's 4,145. Adding two zeros makes it 414,500.\n\nNow, add all three partial products:\n\nFirst: 1,658\n\nSecond: 16,580\n\nThird: 414,500\n\nAdding first two: 1,658 + 16,580 = 18,238.\n\nThen add to the third: 18,238 + 414,500 = 432,738.\n\nWait, let me check that again step by step.\n\nFirst partial product: 829 \u00d7 2 = 1,658.\n\nSecond partial product: 829 \u00d7 20 = 16,580.\n\nThird partial product: 829 \u00d7 500 = 414,500.\n\nAdding them:\n\n1,658 + 16,580 = 18,238.\n\n18,238 + 414,500 = 432,738.\n\nHmm, but let me verify using another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property:\n\n829 \u00d7 522 = 829 \u00d7 (500 + 20 + 2) = 829\u00d7500 + 829\u00d720 + 829\u00d72, which is exactly what I did above. So that gives the same result: 432,738.\n\nAlternatively, let's do the multiplication vertically again to confirm.\n\n\u2003\u2003\u2003829\n\u00d7\u2003522\n--------\nFirst line: 829 \u00d7 2 = 1,658\n\nSecond line: 829 \u00d7 20 = 16,580 (shifted one left)\n\nThird line: 829 \u00d7 500 = 414,500 (shifted two left)\n\nAdding them:\n\n1,658\n\n+16,580\n\n= 18,238\n\n18,238 + 414,500 = 432,738.\n\nYes, that seems consistent.\n\nWait, but let me check with another approach. Let's use the standard algorithm step-by-step:\n\nMultiply 829 by 522.\n\nStart with units digit (2):\n\n829 \u00d7 2:\n\n9\u00d72=18, write 8, carryover 1\n\n2\u00d72=4 +1=5\n\n8\u00d72=16, write 6, carryover 1\n\nBring down the 1: 1,658.\n\nNext digit (tens place, which is 2):\n\n829 \u00d7 2 (but shifted one left):\n\nSame as above, but shifted: 16,580.\n\nThird digit (hundreds place, which is 5):\n\n829 \u00d75 (shifted two left):\n\n9\u00d75=45, write 5 carryover4\n\n2\u00d75=10 +4=14, write4 carryover1\n\n8\u00d75=40 +1=41, write1 carryover4\n\nBring down the 4: 4,145. Shifted two places: 414,500.\n\nAdding all together:\n\n1,658 +16,580 = 18,238\n\n18,238 +414,500 = 432,738.\n\nTherefore, the product of 829 and 522 is 432,738.\n\nWait, but just to ensure there's no arithmetic mistake, let me do a quick check using estimation.\n\n829 is approximately 800, and 522 is approximately 500. Their product is 800\u00d7500=400,000. The actual result, 432,738, is about 32,738 more, which makes sense because both numbers are a bit higher than the estimates. So that seems reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, using another verification method, let's compute 829 \u00d7 522 using another decomposition.\n\nLet me write 829 \u00d7 522 = 829 \u00d7 (500 + 22) = 829\u00d7500 + 829\u00d722.\n\nFirst part: 829\u00d7500 = 414,500.\n\nSecond part: 829\u00d722 = 829\u00d7(20 +2) = 829\u00d720 + 829\u00d72 = 16,580 +1,658 = 18,238.\n\nAdding them together: 414,500 +18,238 = 432,738.\n\nSame result again. I think that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, using the difference of squares or other algebraic identities?\n\nHmm, perhaps not straightforward here. Alternatively, let me compute 800\u00d7522 + 29\u00d7522.\n\n800\u00d7522 = 417,600.\n\n29\u00d7522: Let's compute 30\u00d7522 - 1\u00d7522 = 15,660 - 522 = 15,138.\n\nAdding those: 417,600 +15,138 = 432,738. Again the same result.\n\nAlright, I think that's thorough. I initially messed up by starting with a different problem but caught myself and corrected. The answer should be 432,738.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 829 and 522 is \\boxed{432738}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 829 and 522, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm step by step.\n\nFirst, we multiply 829 by the units digit of 522 (which is 2):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   829 \\\\\n\\times \\quad 2 \\\\\n\\hline\n  1658 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThis gives the first partial product: 1,658.\n\nNext, we multiply 829 by the tens digit of 522 (which is 2, representing 20), and shift the result one position to the left:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   829 \\\\\n\\times 20 \\\\\n\\hline\n 16580 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThis gives the second partial product: 16,580.\n\nThen, we multiply 829 by the hundreds digit of 522 (which is 5, representing 500), and shift the result two positions to the left:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   829 \\\\\n\\times 500 \\\\\n\\hline\n414500 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThis gives the third partial product: 414,500.\n\nAdding all the partial products together:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   1658 \\\\\n+ 16580 \\\\\n+414500 \\\\\n\\hline\n432738 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 829 and 522 is \\(\\boxed{432738}\\).",
