ee zeros because 361 is three digits).\n\nThen multiply 361 by 2 (tens place, which is actually 20):\n\n361 \u00d7 2 = 722, then since it's the tens place, add a zero: 7220.\n\nThen multiply by the next digit, which is 2 (hundreds place of 220, which is 200):\n\n361 \u00d7 2 = 722, then add two zeros: 72,200.\n\nNow add the two results (ignoring the first line which was zero):\n\n7220  \n+72200  \n--------  \nAdding these:\n\n7220  \n+72200  \n= 79420.\n\nYes, same result.\n\nAlternatively, if I use a calculator, 361 \u00d7 220 = ?\n\nWell, I can compute 360 \u00d7 220 + 1 \u00d7 220.\n\n360 \u00d7 220 = (36 \u00d7 22) \u00d7 100. 36 \u00d7 20 = 720, 36 \u00d7 2 = 72; total 720 +72 = 792. So 792 \u00d7 100 = 79,200.\n\nThen 1 \u00d7 220 = 220. So total 79,200 + 220 = 79,420.\n\nSame answer. So confident it's 79,420.\n\nWait, but let me check with another approach to be thorough.\n\nSuppose I do 220 \u00d7 300 = 66,000.\n\n220 \u00d7 60 = 13,200.\n\n220 \u00d7 1 = 220.\n\nAdding those: 66,000 + 13,200 = 79,200 + 220 = 79,420.\n\nYep, same.\n\nAlternatively, breaking down both numbers into their prime factors? Maybe overcomplicating, but let's see:\n\n361 is a known square number. 19 \u00d7 19 = 361. So 361 = 19\u00b2.\n\n220 is 22 \u00d7 10, which is 2 \u00d7 11 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 5, so 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11.\n\nSo 361 \u00d7 220 = 19\u00b2 \u00d7 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11. But not sure if that helps in calculation, but confirms factors.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the identity (a+b)(a-b) but not sure.\n\nAlternatively, use the difference of squares or something else, but not necessary here.\n\nAlternatively, verify the answer by division:\n\nIf 361 \u00d7 220 = 79,420, then 79,420 \u00f7 220 should equal 361.\n\nLet me check:\n\nDivide 79,420 by 220.\n\nFirst, 220 \u00d7 300 = 66,000.\n\n79,420 - 66,000 = 13,420.\n\n220 \u00d7 60 = 13,200.\n\n13,420 - 13,200 = 220.\n\n220 \u00d7 1 = 220.\n\nSo total is 300 + 60 +1 = 361. Perfect, that checks out.\n\nTherefore