      "text": "298, 333.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused here. Let me start over step by step.\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in setting up the partial products. Let me try again.\n\nThe problem was 224 \u00d7 887. Let me confirm the numbers first. Wait, the user asked for 224 times 887, but in my thinking above, I started writing about 196 \u00d7 749 and 589 \u00d7 1. Maybe I got mixed up. Let me check the original question again.\n\nWait, no, the user's question is \"What is the Product of 224 and 887?\" So the problem is 224 \u00d7 887. But in my initial thinking, I was thinking of another problem. Oops, that's a mistake. So I need to correct that.\n\nAlright, let's start over properly.\n\nFirst, let's compute 224 multiplied by 887.\n\nTo do this, I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me write it down step by step.\n\nFirst, write the two numbers vertically aligned:\n\n      224\n    \u00d7 887\n    ---------\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the second number (887), which is 7. Multiply 224 by 7.\n\n224 \u00d7 7:\n\n4 \u00d7 7 = 28, write down 8 and carry over 2.\n\n2 \u00d7 7 = 14 plus the carried 2 = 16, write down 6 and carry over 1.\n\n2 \u00d7 7 = 14 plus the carried 1 = 15. So the first partial product is 1568.\n\nNext, move to the next digit in 887, which is 8 (the tens place). Multiply 224 by 8, but since this is the tens place, we need to shift one position to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 80).\n\n224 \u00d7 8:\n\n4 \u00d7 8 = 32, write down 2 and carry over 3.\n\n2 \u00d7 8 = 16 plus 3 = 19, write down 9 and carry over 1.\n\n2 \u00d7 8 = 16 plus 1 = 17. So the second partial product is 1792, but shifted one place to the left, making it 17,920 (since it's the tens place).\n\nWait, actually, when you shift left by one, it's like multiplying by 10, so 1792 \u00d7 10 = 17,920. That seems right.\n\nNow, moving to the next digit in 887, which is the 8 in the hundreds place. So we multiply 224 by 8 again, but this time shifted two places to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 800).\n\n224 \u00d7 8 is again 1,792, but shifted two places to the left, so 1,792 \u00d7 100 = 179,200.\n\nWait, actually, the hundreds digit is 8, so it should be 224 \u00d7 800. So 224 \u00d7 8 \u00d7 100. Since 224 \u00d78 = 1,792, so 1,792 \u00d7 100 = 179,200. Correct.\n\nNow, we have three partial products:\n\n1. 1,568 (from the units place)\n2. 17,920 (from the tens place)\n3. 179,200 (from the hundreds place)\n\nNow, we add them all together.\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:\n\n    1,568\n  +17,920\n  +179,200\n  -----------\n\nStarting with the first two:\n\n1,568 + 17,920:\n\n1,568 + 17,920. Let's add units:\n\n8 + 0 = 8\n\n6 + 2 = 8\n\n5 + 9 = 14, write down 4 and carry over 1.\n\n1 (carry) + 1 + 7 = 9\n\nSo that gives 19,488. Wait, actually, maybe better to compute:\n\n1,568 + 17,920:\n\n1,568 + 10,000 = 11,568\n\n11,568 + 7,920 = 19,488. Yes, that's correct.\n\nNow add the third partial product: 19,488 + 179,200.\n\n19,488 + 179,200:\n\n19,488 + 160,000 = 179,488\n\n179,488 + 19,200 = 198,688? Wait, no, that can't be right. Wait, 179,200 is the third number. Let's do it step by step.\n\n19,488\n\n+179,200\n\n--------\n\nAdd the thousands:\n\nThe first number is 19,488. The second is 179,200.\n\nAdding the units:\n\n8 + 0 =8\n\n8 +0 =8\n\n4 + 2 =6 (hundreds place)\n\n9 (thousands place) +9 (from 179,200's thousands place?) Wait, let's break it down:\n\n19,488 can be written as 10,000 + 9,000 + 400 + 80 +8\n\n179,200 is 100,000 +70,000 +9,000 +200.\n\nAdding them together:\n\n100,000\n\n+70,000\n\n+9,000 (from 179k) +9,000 (from 19k) = total 18,000? Wait this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, line them up:\n\n\u202f 19,488\n\n+179,200\n\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits place: 8 + 0 =8\n\nTens: 8 +0=8\n\nHundreds: 4 +2=6\n\nThousands: 9 (from 19,488's thousands) +9 (from 179,200's thousands) = 18, so write down 8 and carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 1 (from 19,488's ten-thousands)