e in my thought process. But I caught that and corrected myself. So the final answer should be 17,780.\n\nJust to ensure there's no arithmetic mistake, let me do 140\u00d7127 once again step by step.\n\nCalculating 140 \u00d7 127:\n\nBreak down 127 into 100 + 20 +7.\n\n140\u00d7100 = 14,000\n\n140\u00d720 = 2,800\n\n140\u00d77 = 980\n\nAdding those three:\n\n14,000 + 2,800 = 16,800\n\n16,800 + 980 = 17,780.\n\nYes, that's consistent. Alternatively, perhaps using another breakdown: 140\u00d7127 = 140\u00d7(130-3) = 140\u00d7130 - 140\u00d73.\n\n140\u00d7130: 140\u00d7100=14,000; 140\u00d730=4,200 \u2192 total 18,200.\n\nThen subtract 140\u00d73=420. 18,200 - 420 = 17,780. Same result.\n\nAlright, all methods lead to the same answer, so I'm confident that 17,780 is correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 140 and 127 is \\boxed{17780}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 140 and 127, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller parts for easier calculation.\n\nFirst, we can express 127 as \\(100 + 20 + 7\\):\n\n\\[\n140 \\times 127 = 140 \\times (100 + 20 + 7)\n\\]\n\nNext, we multiply 140 by each part separately:\n\n1. Calculate \\(140 \\times 100\\):\n   \\[\n   140 \\times 100 = 14,000\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(140 \\times 20\\):\n   \\[\n   140 \\times 20 = 2,800\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(140 \\times 7\\):\n   \\[\n   140 \\times 7 = 980\n   \\]\n\nNow, add the results of these three products together:\n\n\\[\n14,000 + 2,800 + 980\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(14,000 + 2,800\\):\n\\[\n14,000 + 2,800 = 16,800\n\\]\n\nThen, add the result to 980:\n\\[\n16,800 + 980 = 17,780\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 140 and 127 is \\(\\boxed{17780}\\).",
