hm again but in a different order.\n\nAlternatively, multiplying 452 \u00d7626.\n\n452 \u00d7600 =271,200.\n\n452 \u00d720 =9,040.\n\n452 \u00d76 =2,712.\n\nAdding those: 271,200 +9,040 =280,240 +2,712 =282,952.\n\nAll methods give the same result, so I think that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the product of 626 and 452 is 282,952.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 626 and 452 is \\boxed{282952}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 626 and 452, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 626 by the units digit of 452 (2):**\n   \\[\n   626 \\times 2 = 1252\n   \\]\n   Partial product: 1252\n\n2. **Multiply 626 by the tens digit of 452 (5), shifted one place to the left:**\n   \\[\n   626 \\times 5 = 3130\n   \\]\n   Shifted partial product: 31300\n\n3. **Multiply 626 by the hundreds digit of 452 (4), shifted two places to the left:**\n   \\[\n   626 \\times 4 = 2504\n   \\]\n   Shifted partial product: 250400\n\n4. **Add all the partial products:**\n   \\[\n   1252 + 31300 + 250400 = 282952\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n- Break down 452 into 400 + 50 + 2 and multiply each part by 626:\n  \\[\n  626 \\times 400 = 250400\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  626 \\times 50 = 31300\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  626 \\times 2 = 1252\n  \\]\n- Sum these results:\n  \\[\n  250400 + 31300 + 1252 = 282952\n  \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 626 and 452 is \\boxed{282952}.",
