ul.\n\nAlternatively, 375 \u00d7775 = 375\u00d7(700 + 75) as before, which gave 290,625. Let me verify with standard multiplication:\n\n   375\n\u00d7 775\n--------\n\nFirst multiply 375 by 5: 1,875\n\nThen multiply 375 by 70: 375\u00d77=2,625, so times 10 is 26,250\n\nThen multiply 375 by 700: 375\u00d77=2,625, times 100 is 262,500\n\nNow add them all together:\n\n1,875 (from 5)\n+26,250 (from 70) = 28,125\n+262,500 (from 700) = 290,625. Same answer. \n\nOkay, so that must be the correct result. So the product of 375 and 775 is 290,625. \n\nWait, but in my first attempt I was confused because I thought the numbers were different, but after realizing the correct numbers, that process worked. I think that's right. Let me confirm with another approach. Let's use calculator steps:\n\n375 \u00d7 700 = 262,500\n\n375 \u00d7 75 = ?\n\nWell 375 \u00d7 75:\n\n375 \u00d7 70 = 26,250\n\n375 \u00d75 = 1,875\n\n26,250 +1,875 = 28,125\n\nAdd to 262,500: 262,500 +28,125 = 290,625. Yep, same answer. \n\nAlternatively, using exponents:\n\n375 \u00d7775 = 375\u00d7(700 + 75) = 375\u00d7700 + 375\u00d775 = 262,500 +28,125 =290,625. \n\nTherefore, the final answer is 290,625. So I think that's correct. I made some confusion at the start but corrected myself. The key was to break down the numbers into manageable parts and then add the results. Alternatively, using standard multiplication step by step also leads to the same result, so I'm confident this is right.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 375 and 775 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n1. **Break down 775 into 700 + 75**:\n   \\[\n   375 \\times 775 = 375 \\times (700 + 75)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply each part separately**:\n   - \\( 375 \\times 700 = 262,\\!500 \\)\n   - \\( 375 \\times 75 = 28,\\!125 \\)\n\n3. **Add the results**:\n   \\[\n   262,\\!500 + 28,\\!125 = 290,\\!625\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer**: \\(\\boxed{290625}\\)",
