ches the previous result.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 539,922. So I must have made an error in my first vertical multiplication approach. Probably messed up the partial products or their placement. Let me try the vertical method again more carefully.\n\nSetting it up:\n\n\u2003\u2003642\n\n\u00d7\u00a0841\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 642 by 1 (units place):\n\n642 \u00d7 1 = 642.\n\nNext, multiply 642 by 4 (tens place). Since it's the tens place, we shift one position left:\n\n642 \u00d7 4 = 2,568. So written as 25,680.\n\nThen multiply 642 by 8 (hundreds place). Shift two positions left:\n\n642 \u00d7 8 = 5,136. So written as 513,600.\n\nNow add them up:\n\n\u2003\u00a0\u00a0642\n\n+25,680\n\n+513,600\n\n--------\n\nFirst, add 642 + 25,680 = 26,322.\n\nThen add 26,322 + 513,600 = 539,922.\n\nYes, that's consistent. So the correct answer is 539,922.\n\nI think in my first attempt I might have miscalculated the partial products or shifted incorrectly. It's important to carefully track each step. So the final answer is 539,922.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 642 and 841 is \\boxed{539922}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 642 and 841, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 642 by 1 (units place):**\n   \\[\n   642 \\times 1 = 642\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 642 by 4 (tens place):**\n   \\[\n   642 \\times 4 = 2568\n   \\]\n   Since this is the tens place, we shift the result one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   25680\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 642 by 8 (hundreds place):**\n   \\[\n   642 \\times 8 = 5136\n   \\]\n   Since this is the hundreds place, we shift the result two positions to the left:\n   \\[\n   513600\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add all the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\phantom{0}642 \\\\\n    +25680 \\\\\n    +513600 \\\\\n    \\hline\n     539922 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 642 and 841 is \\(\\boxed{539922}\\).",
