ght, all methods are leading me to 56,440. Therefore, I think that is the correct answer.\n\nWait, but let me double-check with another approach. Let me do 166 * 340:\n\nBreaking down 166 into 100 + 60 +6.\n\n166*300 = 49,800\n\n166*40 = 6,640\n\nAdding them together: 49,800 +6,640 =56,440. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, 166*340 is same as 340*166. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard multiplication vertically:\n\nMultiply 166 by 340.\n\nBut since 340 is the larger number, arranging them as:\n\n     166\n   x340\n   -------\nProceeding:\n\nFirst, multiply 166 by 0 (units place of 340) \u2192 000.\n\nThen multiply 166 by 4 (tens place of 340, which is 40). 166*4=664, shifted one position to the left \u2192 6,640.\n\nThen multiply 166 by 3 (hundreds place of 340, which is 300). 166*3=498, shifted two positions left \u2192 49,800.\n\nAdd them together:\n\n   000\n\n+6,640\n\n+49,800\n\n=56,440. Correct.\n\nTherefore, after verifying through multiple methods, I'm confident that the product of 340 and 166 is 56,440. I think the confusion earlier was because I started solving a different problem, but now I focused and checked multiple times, so this should be right.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 340 and 166 is \\boxed{56440}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 340 and 166, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify through multiple methods:\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication Algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 340 by 6 (ones place of 166):\n     \\[\n     340 \\times 6 = 2040\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 340 by 60 (tens place of 166):\n     \\[\n     340 \\times 60 = 20400\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 340 by 100 (hundreds place of 166):\n     \\[\n     340 \\times 100 = 34000\n     \\]\n   - Add the partial products together:\n     \\[\n     2040 + 20400 + 34000 = 56440\n     \\]\n\n2. **Distributive Property:**\n   - Break down 166 into 100, 60, and 6:\n     \\[\n     340 \\times 166 = 340 \\times (100 + 60 + 6)\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\