 916 by the hundreds digit (8), shifted two positions to the left:\n\n916 \u00d7 800 = 732,800.\n\nAdding all three:\n\n916\n\n+9,160\n\n+732,800\n\n= 916 + 9,160 = 10,076\n\n10,076 + 732,800 = 742,876.\n\nSame result as before. That seems consistent.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let me verify using another approach, perhaps the difference of squares or another method.\n\nAlternatively, notice that 811 is close to 800, so 916 \u00d7 (800 + 11) = 916 \u00d7 800 + 916 \u00d7 11.\n\nWait, but maybe that complicates. Alternatively, let me try multiplying 916 \u00d7 811 directly step by step.\n\nLet me do the multiplication step by step as if doing it manually:\n\n       916.\n\n     \u00d7811.\n\n------------\n\nFirst, write 916 \u00d7 1 = 916.\n\nThen, 916 \u00d7 1 (tens place) shifted one position left: 916 \u00d7 10 = 9,160.\n\nThen, 916 \u00d7 8 (hundreds place) shifted two positions left: 916 \u00d7 800 = 732,800.\n\nAdding those three gives 916 + 9,160 = 10,076; then 10,076 + 732,800 = 742,876.\n\nAlternatively, let's do it digit by digit:\n\nMultiplying 916 by each digit of 811:\n\nStarting with the units digit (1):\n\n916 \u00d7 1 = 916.\n\nTens digit (1):\n\n916 \u00d7 1 (tens place) is 916 \u00d7 10 = 9,160.\n\nHundreds digit (8):\n\n916 \u00d7 8 (hundreds place) is 916 \u00d7 800 = 732,800.\n\nAdding them all up gives the same result.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the standard algorithm step-by-step:\n\nMultiply 916 by 1 (units):\n\n916 \u00d7 1 = 916.\n\nWrite that down.\n\nThen multiply 916 by 1 (tens place), so it's 916 \u00d7 1, but shifted one to the left:\n\nSo that's 9,160.\n\nThen multiply 916 by 8 (hundreds place), shifted two to the left:\n\n916 \u00d7 8 = 7,328, so with two zeros, that's 732,800.\n\nAdding all three:\n\n916\n\n+9,160\n\n= 10,076\n\nThen adding 732,800 gives 742,876.\n\nAlternatively, to cross-verify, maybe use another multiplication method like the lattice method or the box method.\n\nLet me try the box method:\n\nBreak down each number into hundreds, tens, and ones.\n\n916 = 900 + 10 + 6\n\n811 = 800 + 10 + 1\n\nCreate a 3x3 grid:\n\n        800      10       1\n\n900  900\u00d7800  900\u00d710   900\u00d71\n\n10     10\u00d7800   10\u00d710    10\u00d71\n\n6      6\u00d7800     6\u00d710     6\u00d71\n\nNow compute each cell:\n\nFirst row:\n\n900\u00d7800 = 720,000\n\n900\u00d710 = 9,000\n\n900\u00d71 = 900\n\nSecond row:\n\n10\u00d7800 = 8,000\n\n10\u00d710 = 100\n\n10\u00d71 = 10\n\nThird row:\n\n6\u00d7800 = 4,800\n\n6\u00d710 = 60\n\n6\u00d71 = 6\n\nNow add all these products together:\n\n720,000 + 9,000 + 900 + 8,000 + 100 + 10 + 4,800 + 60 + 6.\n\nLet's add step by step:\n\nStart with 720,000.\n\nAdd 9,000: 729,000.\n\nAdd 900: 729,900.\n\nAdd 8,000: 737,900.\n\nAdd 100: 738,000.\n\nAdd 10: 738,010.\n\nAdd 4,800: 742,810.\n\nAdd 60: 742,870.\n\nAdd 6: 742,876.\n\nSame result again. So that's a good sign.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do it another way, like adding 916 eight hundred times, but that would be tedious.\n\nAlternatively, check using a calculator if possible, but since I can't here, let me see if there's another cross-verification.\n\nLet me compute 916 \u00d7 800 = 732,800\n\nThen 916 \u00d7 11 = 10,076 (since 916\u00d710 +916\u00d71 = 9160+916=10,076)\n\nWait, so 800 + 11 = 811, so total is 732,800 +10,076 = 742,876. Yep, same.\n\nAlternatively, compute 811 \u00d7 900 = 729,900\n\n811 \u00d7 16 = ?\n\nWait, 916 is 900 +16, so 811\u00d7900 +811\u00d716.\n\nCompute 811\u00d7900 = 729,900\n\n811\u00d716: 800\u00d716=12,800; 11\u00d716=176 \u2192 12,800+176=12,976.\n\nAdd 729,900 +12,976 = 742,876. Same answer.\n\nAlright, so all methods are converging to 742,876. I think that's the correct product.\n\nBut just to be absolutely sure, let me try another approach. Let me use the distributive property with different breakdowns.\n\nSuppose I write 811 as 800 + 11. Then:\n\n916 \u00d7 811 = 916 \u00d7 (800 + 11) = 916\u00d7800 + 916\u00d711.\n\nWe already know 916\u00d7800 is 732,800.\