.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the initial standard algorithm steps because I was using different numbers. Wait, the user asked for 480 \u00d7 269, but in my first thought process, I started talking about 247 \u00d7 390 and other numbers. That was a mistake. I must have been distracted. So the correct problem is 480 and 269. So focusing on that, the correct answer is indeed 129,120. Therefore, the final answer should be 129,120.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 480 and 269 is \\boxed{129120}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 480 and 269, we can use the standard algorithm and break down the multiplication into smaller parts:\n\n1. **Multiply 480 by the units digit (9):**\n   \\[\n   480 \\times 9 = 4320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 480 by the tens digit (60):**\n   \\[\n   480 \\times 60 = 28800\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 480 by the hundreds digit (200):**\n   \\[\n   480 \\times 200 = 96000\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add all the results together:**\n   \\[\n   4320 + 28800 + 96000\n   \\]\n\n   - First addition:\n     \\[\n     4320 + 28800 = 33120\n     \\]\n   - Second addition:\n     \\[\n     33120 + 96000 = 129120\n     \\]\n\nThus, the product of 480 and 269 is \\(\\boxed{129120}\\).",
