2,500 =132,500\n\n132,500 +6,200 =138,700\n\n138,700 +1,860 =140,560\n\n140,560 +155 =140,715. Same result.\n\nThus, the product is indeed 140,715.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 140,715.\n\nBut let me just check once more to be thorough. Maybe I can approximate to see if this makes sense.\n\n531 is approximately 500, 265 is approximately 270.\n\n500\u00d7270=135,000. The actual result is 140,715, which is a bit higher, which makes sense because both numbers are slightly higher than the approximations. 531 is 31 more than 500, and 265 is 5 less than 270. The extra from the 531 would be 31\u00d7265 = 8,215 and the loss from 265 being less by 5 is 5\u00d7500=2,500, so net gain is around 5,715, which added to 135,000 gives ~140,715. So that checks out.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident the answer is 140,715.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 531 and 265 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Step-by-Step Calculation:**\n\n1. **Multiply 531 by the units digit (5):**\n   ```\n     531\n   \u00d7   5\n   ------\n    2655\n   ```\n   (5 \u00d7 1 = 5, 5 \u00d7 3 = 15, 5 \u00d7 5 = 25 + carryover 1 = 26)\n\n2. **Multiply 531 by the tens digit (6), shifted one place left:**\n   ```\n     531\n   \u00d7  60\n   ------\n   31860\n   ```\n   (6 \u00d7 1 = 6, 6 \u00d7 3 = 18, 6 \u00d7 5 = 30 + carryover 1 = 31; shifted left by 1)\n\n3. **Multiply 531 by the hundreds digit (2), shifted two places left:**\n   ```\n     531\n   \u00d7 200\n   ------\n  106200\n   ```\n   (2 \u00d7 1 = 2, 2 \u00d7 3 = 6, 2 \u00d7 5 = 10; shifted left by 2)\n\n4. **Add all partial products:**\n   ```\n     2655\n   + 31860\n   +106200\n   ---------\n    140715\n   ```\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 531 and 265 is **140,715**.  \n\n$\\boxed{140715}$",
