g 797 \u00d7551. That must be a mistake. Oh no! I must have misread the numbers. Let me start over.\n\nThe actual problem is 223 multiplied by 860. Let me correct myself.\n\nAlright, so 223 \u00d7 860. Let me approach this carefully.\n\nFirst, note that 860 is 800 + 60. So perhaps breaking it down.\n\n223 \u00d7 860 = 223 \u00d7 (800 + 60) = 223\u00d7800 + 223\u00d760.\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n223\u00d7800: 223 \u00d7 8 \u00d7 100. 223\u00d78 is?\n\n223 \u00d7 8:\n\n200\u00d78=1600\n\n20\u00d78=160\n\n3\u00d78=24\n\nAdding them: 1600 + 160 = 1760; 1760 +24=1784. So 223\u00d78=1784. Hence, 223\u00d7800=1784\u00d7100=178,400.\n\nNext, 223\u00d760: 223\u00d76\u00d710. 223\u00d76:\n\n200\u00d76=1200\n\n20\u00d76=120\n\n3\u00d76=18\n\nAdding those: 1200+120=1320; +18=1338. So 223\u00d76=1338. Then \u00d710 is 13,380.\n\nNow add 178,400 + 13,380.\n\nAdding them together:\n\n178,400 + 10,000 = 188,400\n\n188,400 + 3,380 = 191,780.\n\nWait, let me verify step by step:\n\n178,400\n\n+ 13,380\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the thousands:\n\n178,400 + 13,000 = 191,400\n\nThen add 380: 191,400 + 380 = 191,780.\n\nTherefore, the product of 223 and 860 is 191,780.\n\nAlternatively, using standard multiplication:\n\n     223\n\n   \u00d7860\n\n   --------\n\nFirst, multiply 223 by 0 (units place of 860), which gives 0. Since it's the units place, we can just write 0 or skip it.\n\nNext, multiply 223 by 6 (tens place of 860, which is actually 60). So 223 \u00d76 = 1,338. Since it's the tens place, we shift one position to the left, so 13,380.\n\nThen multiply 223 by 8 (hundreds place of 860, which is 800). 223\u00d78=1,784, shifted two positions to the left becomes 178,400.\n\nAdding the two partial products:\n\n178,400\n\n+ 13,380\n\n= 191,780. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps even another way: 223 \u00d7 860.\n\nNote that 860 is 86 \u00d710, so 223\u00d786\u00d710. Let's compute 223\u00d786 first, then multiply by 10.\n\nCalculate 223\u00d786:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n223\u00d780 + 223\u00d7