t approach using the standard algorithm, I got 79,785, and the other methods also confirm it. Therefore, the correct answer should be 79,785.\n\nHowever, I need to check if the user's original question was indeed 135 \u00d7 591. The user wrote:\n\n\"Question: What is the Product of 135 and 591?\"\n\nYes, that's correct. So the thought process initially went off track with different numbers, but upon refocusing, the correct answer is 79,785.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 135 and 591 is \\boxed{79785}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 135 and 591, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write the numbers vertically:\n   ```\n     135\n   \u00d7 591\n   --------\n   ```\n\n2. Multiply 135 by the units digit of 591 (1):\n   ```\n     135\n   \u00d7    1\n   --------\n     135\n   ```\n\n3. Multiply 135 by the tens digit of 591 (9), shifted one position to the left:\n   ```\n     135\n   \u00d7    9\n   --------\n    1215\n   ```\n   Shifted left by one position: 12150\n\n4. Multiply 135 by the hundreds digit of 591 (5), shifted two positions to the left:\n   ```\n     135\n   \u00d7    5\n   --------\n     675\n   ```\n   Shifted left by two positions: 67500\n\n5. Add all three partial products together:\n   ```\n      135\n    +12150\n    +67500\n   --------\n     79785\n   ```\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n- Break down the multiplication: \\(135 \\times 591 = 135 \\times (600 - 9)\\)\n- Calculate each term:\n  - \\(135 \\times 600 = 81000\\)\n  - \\(135 \\times 9 = 1215\\)\n- Subtract the results: \\(81000 - 1215 = 79785\\)\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 135 and 591 is \\(\\boxed{79785}\\).",
