5,800 to that: 51,336 +55,800 = 107,136?\n\nWait, 51,336 + 55,800:\n\n51,336 +50,000 =101,336\n\nThen +5,800 =107,136. Yes.\n\nSo the total product is 107,136. Is that correct?\n\nLet me check with another method to verify.\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property:\n\n558 \u00d7192 =558\u00d7(200 -8)=558\u00d7200 -558\u00d78.\n\nCalculate each part:\n\n558\u00d7200 =558\u00d72\u00d7100=1,116\u00d7100=111,600\n\n558\u00d78=4,464\n\nSo subtract: 111,600 -4,464 = 107,136. Same result. Okay, that matches. So the answer is 107,136.\n\nBut let me confirm once more with direct multiplication.\n\nAnother way: 558 \u00d7 192.\n\nBreak down 192 into 100+90+2. So:\n\n558\u00d7100 =55,800\n\n558\u00d790=50,220 (since 558\u00d79=5,022, so \u00d710 gives 50,220)\n\n558\u00d72=1,116\n\nAdding them up:55,800 +50,220 =106,020; 106,020 +1,116 =107,136. Yep, same answer. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the product of 558 and 192 is 107,136.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 558 and 192 is \\boxed{107136}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 558 and 192, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 558 by the units digit of 192 (2):**\n   \\[\n   558 \\times 2 = 1116\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 558 by the tens digit of 192 (9), which is actually 90. Then shift the result one place to the left:**\n   \\[\n   558 \\times 9 = 5022 \\quad \\text{(then shift left by one place)} \\quad 50220\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 558 by the hundreds digit of 192 (1), which is actually 100. Then shift the result two places to the left:**\n   \\[\n   558 \\times 1 = 558 \\quad \\text{(then shift left by two places)} \\quad 55800\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add all the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   1116 + 50220 + 55800\n   \\]\n   - First, add 1116 and 50220:\n     \\[\n     1116 + 50220 = 51336\n     \\]\n   - Then, add the result to 55800:\n     \\[\n     51336 + 55800 = 107136\n     \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by breaking down 192 into \\(200 - 8\\):\n   \\[\n   558 \\times 192 = 558 \\times (200 - 8) = 558 \\times 200 - 558 \\times 8\n   \\]\n   - Calculate each part:\n     \\[\n     558 \\times 200 = 111600 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 558 \\times 8 = 4464\n     \\]\n   - Subtract the results:\n     \\[\n     111600 - 4464 = 107136\n     \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{107136}\\).",
