r, but since it's zero, the partial product here is 0000 (but shifted one place to the left, so effectively 0).\n\nThen the hundreds place: the 3 in 304 is in the hundreds place, so it's 300. Multiply 667 by 300. Let's do 667 \u00d7 3 first. 600\u00d73=1800, 60\u00d73=180, 7\u00d73=21. Adding those: 1800+180=1980, plus 21 is 2001. Then multiply by 100, so it's 200,100.\n\nNow, add the partial products:\n\nFirst partial product: 2,668\n\nSecond partial product: 0 (but shifted one place, so actually 00,00. Wait, actually, when multiplying by the tens place (which is 0 here), you would multiply by 0 and then shift one position, but since it's zero, the entire partial product is zero. So adding that doesn't change anything.\n\nThird partial product: 200,100 shifted two places? Wait, actually, when multiplying by hundreds place, you shift two places. Wait, let me get this straight.\n\nWait, in standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit (3 in 304), you place the result two positions to the left. So 300 is 3\u00d7100, so the partial product is 667\u00d73\u00d7100 = 2001\u00d7100 = 200,100, which is correctly shifted two places.\n\nSo adding all three partial products:\n\n2,668 (units place)\n\n+ 0 (tens place, but shifted one, so actually 0000 but shifted one, which is still 0)\n\n+ 200,100 (hundreds place, shifted two places: so it's 200,100)\n\nTotal is 2,668 + 200,100 = 202,768.\n\nWait, but let me check that again. 200,100 plus 2,668 is 202,768?\n\nYes, because 200,000 + 2,000 is 202,000, and 100 + 668 is 768, so total 202,768.\n\nHmm, let me verify with another method to be sure. Let's use the distributive property:\n\n667 \u00d7 304 = 667 \u00d7 (300 + 4) = 667\u00d7300 + 667\u00d74\n\nWhich is exactly what I did above. 667\u00d7300=200,100 and 667\u00d74=2,668. Adding those gives 202,768.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps breaking down the numbers further:\n\nLet me try 667 \u00d7 304.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 667 \u00d7 (300 + 4) = 667\u00d7300 + 667\u