\nWait, but let me do it another way to be absolutely sure. Maybe converting to scientific notation?\n\n930 is 9.3 \u00d7 10^2, and 210 is 2.1 \u00d7 10^2.\n\nMultiplying them: (9.3 \u00d7 2.1) \u00d7 10^(2+2) = (9.3 \u00d7 2.1) \u00d7 10^4.\n\nCalculating 9.3 \u00d7 2.1:\n\n9 \u00d7 2 = 18\n\n9 \u00d7 0.1 = 0.9\n\n0.3 \u00d7 2 = 0.6\n\n0.3 \u00d7 0.1 = 0.03\n\nAdding those: 18 + 0.9 + 0.6 + 0.03 = 19.53\n\nTherefore, 19.53 \u00d7 10^4 = 195,300. Perfect.\n\nAlright, so all methods lead to 195,300. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 930 and 210 is 195,300.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 930 and 210 is \\boxed{195300}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 930 and 210, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller parts for easier calculation.\n\nFirst, note that both numbers end in zeros, which simplifies the multiplication. We can separate the non-zero parts and then add the zeros at the end. \n\n1. **Break down the numbers:**\n   - 930 can be written as \\(93 \\times 10\\)\n   - 210 can be written as \\(21 \\times 10\\)\n\n2. **Multiply the non-zero parts:**\n   - Calculate \\(93 \\times 21\\):\n     \\[\n     93 \\times 20 = 1860\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     93 \\times 1 = 93\n     \\]\n     Adding these results:\n     \\[\n     1860 + 93 = 1953\n     \\]\n\n3. **Combine the results and add the zeros:**\n   - Since we separated \\(10\\) from each original number, we have \\(10 \\times 10 = 100\\).\n   - Therefore, multiply the result by \\(100\\):\n     \\[\n     1953 \\times 100 = 195300\n     \\]\n\n4. **Verification using the standard algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 930 by 210 directly:\n     \\[\n     930 \\times 200 = 186000\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     930 \\times 10 = 9300\n     \\]\n     Adding these results:\n     \\[\n     186000 + 9300 = 195300\n     \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 930 and 210 is \\(\\boxed{195300}\\).",
