Yep, that's the same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard algorithm again:\n\n       452\n     \u00d7 397\n     -------\n         3164  (452 \u00d7 7)\n        40680  (452 \u00d7 90)\n      135600   (452 \u00d7 300)\n     -----------\n      179444\n\nAdding those three: 3,164 + 40,680 = 43,844. Then 43,844 + 135,600 = 179,444. All right, so that's consistent. Therefore, I think the product is 179,444. I don't see any mistakes in the calculations now. So the final answer should be 179,444.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 452 and 397 is \\boxed{179444}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 452 and 397, we can use the distributive property and break down the multiplication into smaller parts:\n\n1. **Break down 397 into 400 - 3:**\n   \\[\n   452 \\times 397 = 452 \\times (400 - 3)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate each part separately:**\n   - Compute \\(452 \\times 400\\):\n     \\[\n     452 \\times 400 = 452 \\times 4 \\times 100 = 1808 \\times 100 = 180800\n     \\]\n   - Compute \\(452 \\times 3\\):\n     \\[\n     452 \\times 3 = 1356\n     \\]\n\n3. **Subtract the two results:**\n   \\[\n   180800 - 1356 = 179444\n   \\]\n\n4. **Verification using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 452 by 7:\n     \\[\n     452 \\times 7 = 3164\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 452 by 90 (shifted one position to the left):\n     \\[\n     452 \\times 90 = 40680\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 452 by 300 (shifted two positions to the left):\n     \\[\n     452 \\times 300 = 135600\n     \\]\n   - Add the results together:\n     \\[\n     3164 + 40680 + 135600 = 179444\n     \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 452 and 397 is \\(\\boxed{179444}\\).",
