t errors, especially with larger numbers. Using multiple methods to verify the result is a good strategy to ensure accuracy.\n\nAnother way to check could be using estimation. If I round 420 to 400 and 508 to 500, their product is 200,000. The actual answer should be a bit higher since both numbers are slightly larger than the rounded values. 213,360 is indeed higher than 200,000, which makes sense. The difference comes from the extra 20 in 420 and 8 in 508, so the estimation aligns with the exact calculation.\n\nAdditionally, if I use a calculator to verify, 420 multiplied by 508 equals 213,360. Since I don't have a calculator here, but given that multiple manual calculation methods all lead to the same result, I can be confident this is correct.\n\nIn summary, breaking down the multiplication into parts, using the distributive property, and verifying through different methods like the standard algorithm and factoring all confirm that the product is 213,360. It's crucial to stay organized and methodical when dealing with multi-digit multiplication to avoid arithmetic mistakes. Checking each step and cross-verifying with alternative approaches helps ensure the solution's accuracy.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 420 and 508 is \\boxed{213360}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 420 and 508, we can use the distributive property and the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nFirst, breaking down the numbers using the distributive property:\n\\[\n420 \\times 508 = 420 \\times (500 + 8)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 420 \\times 500 + 420 \\times 8\n\\]\nCalculating each term separately:\n\\[\n420 \\times 500 = 210,000\n\\]\n\\[\n420 \\times 8 = 3,360\n\\]\nAdding the results:\n\\[\n210,000 + 3,360 = 213,360\n\\]\n\nUsing the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n    420 \\\\\n\\times 508 \\\\\n\\hline\n    3360 \\quad (\\text{420} \\times 8) \\\\\n  0 \\quad \\quad (\\text{420} \\times 0, \\text{shifted one position left}) \\\\\n210000 \\quad (\\text{420} \\tim